    contentEn: `<p>The trip from Puerto de Mogán to Las Palmas de Gran Canaria takes around 52 minutes to cover roughly 75 km, tracing almost the entire length of the GC-1 motorway from the harbour town at the island's south-western tip up to the capital in the north-east. A private transfer collects you from your hotel or apartment in Puerto de Mogán and drives you directly to your destination in Las Palmas, without changing vehicles or making scheduled stops on the way.</p>
<p>This is a city-to-city journey, not an airport connection, so pickup is arranged around your own schedule rather than a flight time. That flexibility is useful whether you're catching an early train of thought toward a business meeting in the capital, timing your arrival for a cruise departure from Puerto de la Luz, or simply moving on to spend the rest of your trip in a different part of the island.</p>
<p>You can book this transfer through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get a quote</a>, choosing your own pickup time and destination address. If you expect to need transport more than once during your stay, our <a href="/city-to-city-transfers/">city-to-city transfers</a> and hourly chauffeur services both cover this full corridor as many times as your plans require.</p>
<h2>Leaving Little Venice Behind</h2>
<p>Puerto de Mogán, nicknamed "Little Venice of Gran Canaria" for the canals and footbridges around its marina, began life as a working fishing harbour and has grown into a low-rise resort town built directly around the water. Many visitors making this trip north are wrapping up a stay there, perhaps timed around the well-known Friday market held along the harbourfront, and heading to Las Palmas either to continue their journey or to see a genuinely different side of the island before departing.</p>
<p>The drive covers close to the full length of the GC-1 motorway, running along the southern and eastern coastlines before curving into the capital. The road stays fast and well surfaced for almost the entire distance, which keeps the journey comfortable despite being the longest city-to-city route on this corridor. The one point where traffic regularly builds is on the final approach into Las Palmas during the morning and evening rush hours, when local commuter traffic adds to the volume.</p>
<h3>Crossing the Island's Climate Line</h3>
<p>This route also crosses Gran Canaria's clearest climate divide. Puerto de Mogán and the surrounding south-west coast sit in one of the island's driest, sunniest microclimates, while Las Palmas on the north-east coast catches considerably more cloud cover from the Atlantic trade winds. It's entirely normal to leave a sunny morning at the marina in Mogán and arrive in a cooler, more overcast capital under an hour later, especially outside the peak summer months.</p>
<h3>A Different Kind of City</h3>
<p>Las Palmas is a working Spanish city of around 380,000 people, a sharp contrast to the compact, tourism-built character of Puerto de Mogán. It has its own historic quarter in Vegueta, a long urban beach at Las Canteras, and a proper commercial centre around Mesa y López, none of which have an equivalent in the resort towns of the south-west. A private driver takes you to your specific address within whichever of these districts you're headed to, rather than a single central drop-off point, which matters in a spread-out city in a way it doesn't within Mogán's compact harbour area. More detail on the capital is available in our <a href="/las-palmas-de-gran-canaria/">Las Palmas de Gran Canaria</a> guide.</p>
<h3>Making the Most of a Long Drive</h3>
<p>Because this route covers nearly the full length of the island, it's one of the few city-to-city trips on Gran Canaria where the journey itself becomes part of the experience rather than a formality to get through. The road climbs briefly away from the coast near Mogán before rejoining the GC-1 for the long run north, passing through scenery that shifts from the drier, terraced hillsides of the south-west to the greener slopes closer to the capital. A private car lets you take that in properly, without needing to watch the road yourself or manage a change of buses partway through.</p>
<p>If you'd rather use the time productively, the length of this drive also makes it one of the more practical windows on the island for a work call, reviewing plans for the rest of your stay, or simply catching up on rest after a few days spent around the water in Puerto de Mogán. Since the vehicle and driver are yours for the whole journey, there's no schedule to interrupt that flexibility.</p>
<h3>Arriving with Time to Spare</h3>
<p>Travellers connecting onward from Las Palmas, whether to a flight, a cruise at Puerto de la Luz, or an appointment elsewhere in the capital, generally benefit from building in a little extra buffer given the length of this particular route compared to the shorter hops elsewhere on the island. A driver who covers this corridor regularly can advise on realistic timing once they know your onward connection, helping you avoid the tighter margins that a self-driven trip on unfamiliar roads might otherwise involve.</p>
<h3>Why Visit the Capital from Mogán</h3>
<p>Travellers based in Puerto de Mogán for the bulk of their stay sometimes underestimate how different Las Palmas feels from the resort towns of the south-west, and a day or two in the capital is often the only chance on a Gran Canaria trip to see the island's working, historic side rather than its purpose-built tourist side. The Vegueta quarter's fifteenth-century streets and the Santa Ana Cathedral offer a sense of history that a marina town built up over the last few decades doesn't have, and the long stretch of Las Canteras beach, backed by a proper city promenade of cafes and shops, is a different kind of beach day from the calmer, more sheltered water around Mogán's harbour.</p>
<p>For visitors who've spent most of their trip around the water in the south-west, a day in Las Palmas also means access to a wider range of shopping and dining than a small harbour town can support, from the department stores and independent shops around Mesa y López to a broader spread of restaurants serving a resident population as well as tourists.</p>
<h3>Pickup Logistics Around the Harbour</h3>
<p>Puerto de Mogán's accommodation sits close around the marina and the older part of the village just behind it, generally within a short walk of the harbourfront itself, which makes pickup straightforward compared to some of the hillside resorts further along the coast. Giving your specific hotel or apartment building when you book, rather than just "Puerto de Mogán", still helps, since the older streets closest to the water are largely pedestrian and a driver needs to know which access road to use to get as close as possible to your door. Confirming a pickup point near the harbour entrance is usually the simplest option if your accommodation itself sits down one of the narrower pedestrian lanes.</p>
<h2>Planning the Journey North</h2>
<p>Given the length of this route, it's worth thinking about departure timing when you book, particularly if you're aiming to arrive in Las Palmas ahead of a specific commitment such as a cruise departure or flight connection elsewhere on your itinerary. A private transfer removes the need to return a rental car or navigate an unfamiliar city by bus with luggage. For groups making this trip together, our group and event transfers and corporate travel services scale to multi-vehicle bookings, and our wider <a href="/gran-canaria/">Gran Canaria</a> guide covers more of what lies between these two very different points on the island.</p>`,
